      <h1 class="terms-conditions-title">Twennie Terms and Conditions</h1>

      <h2>Twennie Leader and Group Member Policy</h2>
      <h3>Mutual Respect and Professional Conduct</h3>
      <p>Twennie is a collaborative learning community where members share knowledge, insights, and professional experiences. As a leader or group member, you agree to uphold the following standards:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Respect for Others:</strong> Treat fellow members with kindness, respect, and professionalism. Engage in discussions and feedback with a spirit of constructive dialogue.</li>
        <li><strong>Zero Tolerance for Harassment or Abuse:</strong> Twennie does not tolerate harassment, bullying, discrimination, or any form of abusive behavior. This includes but is not limited to offensive language, intimidation, or exclusionary practices.</li>
        <li><strong>Generous Collaboration:</strong> Members are encouraged to engage in collaborative learning exercises in a spirit of generosity, contributing knowledge and support to peers.</li>
        <li><strong>Constructive Feedback:</strong> When providing critiques or comments on learning units, ensure feedback is constructive, relevant, and aligned with the goal of fostering professional growth.</li>
        <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> If conflicts arise, members are expected to resolve them through respectful communication. Any concerns regarding violations of Twennie’s principles should be reported to the administrators.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Twennie’s Statement on Intellectual Property</h2>
      <p>As a member of the Twennie learning community, you can submit your learning to the library. To be allowed to submit to the library, you must first accept these terms and conditions. Your work might be an article, video, or interview you conducted with someone in your industry. It could be a prompt set you wrote, an exercise you created, or a template you designed. It might also be a combination of these units in the form of a mission. It could be a market research nugget you are tracking along with your team. You might conduct internal research and, if it is meant to support your industry and profession, share it on Twennie with all members. On the Twennie site, we refer to these as “learning units.” <br><br>
      Members wishing to add content to the Twennie library must use the unit formats available: articles, videos, interviews, prompt sets, exercises, templates, missions, and nuggets. We do not accept courses or presentations from other plaforms until they have been converted to Twennie unit format.<br><br>If you have opted to create group-only content, your learning units will be accessible to your group only based on the visibility settings you choose when uploading the content. If you have decided to share your learning content with all Twennie members, no learning unit you submit to the library will be used for any purpose other than to support learning and development for other Twennie members unless you consent to promotional use of your content at the time of upload or at a later date.</p>
      <p>As with all content submitted by Twennie members, your work is yours. Unless you wish to submit anonymously, every time your content appears on the Twennie platform or in Twennie emails or promotions, your name will appear with it. You have the option to refuse to allow your work to be used for promotional purposes, which you must indicate at the time of upload. <strong>Submitting to the library in all-member visibility mode implies consent for Twennie administrators and members to access your content for learning purposes.</strong></p>

      <h2>Twennie Approval</h2>
      <p>All content uploaded to the Twennie platform is subject to approval by Twennie administrators. Approval might depend on the quality and teaching potential of the information, its length or word count, or its subject matter. Twennie reserves the right to reject or delete member-posted content for any reason. If your content is rejected, you may contact Twennie directly for guidance on improving and changing the material before resubmitting.</p>
      
      <h2>Twennie Not Liable</h2>
      <p>Be advised that the all-member Twennie library is open to anyone who completes a membership form with a valid email address. At no time will Twennie be held responsible for plagiarism or copyright violations of the work of members once posted on Twennie’s website. If you are concerned about infringement on your intellectual property, we recommend that you do not post it to Twennie in all-member mode. Group-only mode provides greater protection than all-member mode, but we still do not guarantee that your content will be invulnerable to infringement. Highly competitive or sensitive corporate or personal information should not be shared on the Twennie platform under any circumstances, even in organization- or group-only mode.</p>
      
      <h2>Prosecuting Copyright Infringements</h2>
      <p>Any legal prosecution of copyright infringement of the work of Twennie members published on the Twennie website is the sole responsibility of the author and not the responsibility of Twennie or its administrators, owners, or employees.</p>
      
      <h2>Cancellation</h2>
      <p>Upon cancelling your membership, you have the option to request that Twennie delete your submitted content. We humbly ask, however, that you allow Twennie to continue featuring your content in our library based on your visibility settings. Your content forms part of an important body of knowledge, and we want to continue offering that knowledge to members. These terms and conditions confirm that Twennie will never attempt to prevent you from using your own content on other forums or publishing elsewhere in the future, but we would like to continue featuring it in the library even if you decide not to continue your Twennie membership. </p>
      
      <h2>Citations</h2>
      <p>If you wish to cite the work of a Twennie member in any forum other than on the Twennie website, it is your responsibility to obtain permission from the author and instructions on how that author wishes to be cited. Provide the author with a statement of the purpose for which the material will be used and how many times and where it will be featured.</p>
      
      <h2>Twennie Membership Activity Reporting</h2>
      <p><strong>Submitting content to the library implies consent for the material to be incorporated into internal Twennie reports.</strong> This might include but not be limited to records of completed prompts, results of learning progress over time, or membership longevity. This information could be used in Twennie's promotional materials, but your organization or members will not be named specifically without Twennie first obtaining yours or their written permission.</p>
      
      <h2>Competitive and Personal Information</h2>
      <p>Twennie will not accept library units that include the names or contact information of third parties unless the unit comes equipped with written permission from those individuals and/or organizations that can be confirmed directly by Twennie administrators. Links to the web content of member organizations or members are permitted on Twennie, but excessive promotional content is not. Twennie's platform is designed primarily for learning purposes. Promotional content will be approved or rejected according to the discretion of Twennie administrators.</p>
      
      <h2>Alternatives to Sharing Competitive Information</h2>
      <p>If you want to post to the library but would prefer to avoid divulging competitive information, Twennie offers numerous alternatives. You can use all-member, group-only, or organization-only modes for submitting content, depending on the nature of that content. This must be specified at the time of upload. Keep in mind that if you are an individual member, sharing with your group or organization only will NOT be visible to any Twennie members other than you. This is because individual members do not have groups or organizations set up on Twennie.</p>

          <h2>How Twennie Protects Your Work, Knowledge, and Data</h2>
<p>
Those working in consulting, engineering, sciences, project delivery, or a variety of other disciplines, are understandably cautious about protecting their intellectual property and internal knowledge. Twennie is cloud-based, but built with this sensitivity in mind. By giving you the option to set visibility in one of three modes, group-only, organization-only, or all Twennie members, Twennie is committed to protecting your posted content. Please note: Twennie is a <strong>learning platform</strong> — not a document repository. We do not supply any information about one member firm to any other member firm. Neither are we an information-harvesting system. We do not want, request, or accept proprietary materials containing competitive or sensitive information. Any content that (unintentionally or not) contains sensitive information will be rejected by Twennie administrators after it has been posted. For the security of your content, this will not be enforced as strictly in group-only or organization-only visibility mode, but will still be enforced. If you discover that sensitive content has been uploaded before Twennie administrators see it, contact Twennie to request to have it erased from our database. If sensitive content is posted and seen by any members before Twennie has opportunity to remove it, Twennie is not responsible for any associated repercussions or losses. 
</p>

<h3>1. You Own Your Content</h3>
<p>
Anything you write, record, or upload to Twennie remains <strong>your property</strong>. Twennie claims no ownership or rights over the materials or notes you create. You decide whether to share your work with your group, organization, or with all members. You may delete your content at any time, but you must contact Twennie directly to do it. Delete functions are not available to members in the Twennie platform for learning content.
</p>

<h3>2. Twennie Never Requests or Requires Proprietary or Confidential Material</h3>
<p>
Twennie does <strong>not</strong> request or accept: project reports, design files, drawings, client data, bids or proposals, internal documents, financial reports, pricing, names of personnel, or any competitive information. Learning units are skill-based exercises — not submissions of company-sensitive documents. If an assignment or exercise <em>feels</em> like it approaches or hints at confidential material, we expect and encourage you to generalize, anonymize, or reframe the content before posting.
</p>

<h3>3. Your Private Notes Are Not Visible to Twennie</h3>
<p>
Notes you record while completing learning units — including reflections, answers to prompts, or documentation of your own progress — are <strong>visible only to you</strong>, and when applicable, to your team’s designated leader. Twennie administrators do <em>not</em> see your private notes or internal observations unless we are assisting your group leaders in producing reports.
</p>

<h3>4. Twennie Does Not Use Your Content for Any Purpose Without Consent</h3>
<p>
Content you mark as “all-member” may be viewed by other Twennie members for learning purposes only. It will <strong>never</strong> be used in Twennie promotional materials without your explicit consent. Content marked “team-only” remains visible exclusively within your private group unless you manually change those permissions.
</p>

<h3>5. No Competitive Data Is Ever Required or Accepted</h3>
<p>
Twennie learning activities may ask you to think about client experience, communication, project management, or business development habits — but they never require you to upload client names, pricing, strategies, proprietary workflows, or competitive materials. Members are reminded regularly not to share sensitive or regulated information.
</p>

<h3>6. You Control What You Share</h3>
<p>
Every unit on Twennie can be created and shared in three modes: <strong>with your group only</strong>, your <strong>organization</strong> only, or <strong> all members</strong>. You choose the audience for every piece of content you create. 
</p>

<h3>7. Twennie Is Not a Repository for Company IP</h3>
<p>
Twennie is not designed to store or manage company intellectual property and should not be used that way. Companies retain full responsibility for their proprietary information. Twennie encourages members to avoid uploading materials that could be sensitive, competitive, or confidential.
</p>

<h3>8. You May Use Twennie Without Sharing Anything at All</h3>
<p>
Members may use Twennie solely for accessing Twennie-generated learning content without submitting a single unit to the library. A full learning experience is possible without creating or sharing any content.
</p>

<h3>9. Private Mode Is Always the Safest Option</h3>
<p>
If you or your organization have risk concerns related to posting content, we recommend using <strong>group-only mode</strong> for all uploads and generalizing or anonymizing any project details before posting. This mode ensures your work is accessible only to your immediate learning group.
</p>

<h3>10. Twennie Actively Discourages Sharing Sensitive Information</h3>
<p>
Twennie’s review process is designed to prevent inappropriate submissions. Administrators may reject any uploaded content for any reason. This protects both our members and their employer organizations.
</p>

<h3>Your Trust Matters</h3>
<p>
Twennie exists to help technical professionals learn, collaborate, and grow — <em>not</em> to harvest information. Safeguarding your intellectual property is part of our commitment to maintaining a safe, ethical, and professional learning environment. If you ever have concerns about a planned upload, contact us and we will guide you on how to share your work safely.
</p>
